"pa":24.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":-0.0252,"pass_rate":0.5588,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5,"def_epa_per_play":0.03419},{"week":17,"opponent":"LAC","home":true,"pf":7.0,"pa":40.0,"off_snaps":48,"epa_per_play":-0.5519,"pass_rate":0.5833,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5,"def_epa_per_play":0.2911},{"week":18,"opponent":"BUF","home":true,"pf":23.0,"pa":16.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":0.03347,"pass_rate":0.5345,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5769,"def_epa_per_play":-0.06715}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"JAX","distance":0.5813,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6292,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"GB","distance":0.6421,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"},{"season":2025,"team":"CAR","distance":0.6887,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2022,"team":"GB","distance":0.721,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"}],"defense":[{"season":2020,"team":"HOU","distance":0.6744,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6814,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NYG","distance":0.7599,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CAR","distance":0.781,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Ejiro Evero"},{"season":2020,"team":"MIA","distance":0.8032,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Patriots went 4-13 in 2024 and were outscored 417 to 289. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 27th in EPA per play and the defense 29th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd highest RPO rate and the lowest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Patriots offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest RPO rate and a 12 personnel usage that was among the highest in the league. Drake Maye ran the passing game, with Hunter Henry leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Patriots had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 26th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 8th, and its weakest was sack rate, where it ranked 32nd. Keion White led the team with 5 sacks, and Deatrich Wise Jr. added 5 despite playing only 41% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the lowest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Patriots were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 14% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 79% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 20% of the money on the books. Their receivers were among the youngest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Drake Maye and Jacoby Brissett both getting meaningful snaps. Targets were spread around, with Hunter Henry and Demario Douglas leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Rhamondre Stevenson handled most of the work in the backfield with 57% of the carries. On defense, Keion White led the team with 5 sacks and Christian Gonzalez picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Jaguars and the 2022 Steelers. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Texans and the 2020 Eagles.","trends":"The offense went from 31st in EPA per play in 2023 to 27th in 2024, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 12th in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 29th in 2024, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.02 to 6.15.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}